# DPCR-IDS Simulation in OMNeT++ 6.3.0 — Feasibility & Implementation Plan

## Executive Summary

**Yes, it is feasible to simulate your DPCR-IDS in OMNeT++ 6.3.0**, but it requires significant custom module development in C++/NED. OMNeT++ provides the discrete-event simulation backbone and realistic network timing — the two things your current Python-based `simulate_real_model_runtime.py` cannot deliver. Below is the full analysis.

---

## 1. What OMNeT++ Brings vs. Your Current Setup

| Aspect | Current Python Replay | OMNeT++ Simulation |
|--------|----------------------|-------------------|
| **Bus Timing** | Pseudo-timestamps (`pseudo_time_step: 0.01`) | Realistic CAN arbitration delays, Ethernet frame queuing |
| **Multi-ECU Topology** | Single-process sequential replay | True parallel ECU message generation with bus contention |
| **Attack Injection** | Pre-labeled CSV rows | Attacker ECU nodes that inject frames at runtime |
| **Latency Measurement** | Python `time.perf_counter()` on host CPU | Simulated capture-to-decision latency reflecting bus propagation |
| **Gateway Behavior** | `DecisionAggregator` with synthetic timestamps | Actual CAN↔Ethernet gateway forwarding with queuing |
| **Reproducibility** | Seed-based but host-dependent | Fully deterministic discrete-event simulation |

