## 5. Significant Challenges & Honest Assessment

> [!WARNING]
> ### High-Effort Items
> 
> 1. **CAN Feature Extraction in C++**: Your Python pipeline computes `iat_same_id`, `payload_entropy`, `payload_delta_mean`, `msg_freq_hz`, `local_busload`, `bitflip_ratio`. All 16 features must be **reimplemented in C++** inside the OMNeT++ module. This alone is ~2-3 days of work.
> 
> 2. **ONNX Runtime Linking**: Building ONNX Runtime as a shared library and integrating it into the OMNeT++ Makefile system (which uses `opp_makemake`) is non-trivial. Expect ~1-2 days of build configuration work.
> 
> 3. **FiCo4OMNeT Adaptation**: While compatible with OMNeT++ 6, the CAN frame format in FiCo4OMNeT may not directly expose raw payload bytes. You may need to extend the CAN frame data structure.
> 
> 4. **No Pre-built IDS Module Exists**: There is no off-the-shelf IDS module for OMNeT++. Every component listed in Section 3 must be written as a custom `cSimpleModule` in C++ with NED declarations.
> 
> 5. **Validation Parity**: You need to ensure the OMNeT++ simulation produces identical detection results to your Python pipeline on the same input data before claiming any new results.

> [!CAUTION]
> ### Scope vs. Timeline Trade-off
> 
> **Full simulation** (all components, all attacks, bus-load sweep) = **4-8 weeks** of development.  
> **Lightweight simulation** (simplified CAN bus + pre-computed features fed from trace files) = **2-3 weeks**.  
> **Trace-replay only** (OMNeT++ as a timing/queuing wrapper around pre-computed decisions) = **1-2 weeks**.

